sections.s2_technical:
`Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n)` creates a `ThreadPoolExecutor` with a fixed core and maximum of n threads and an unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue`. You use it when you have a known, steady workload and you want predictable memory and CPU use. For example, a settlement service that processes a steady stream of trades: `ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(16);`. The hidden danger is that the queue is unbounded, so if producers outpace consumers the queue grows until the heap is exhausted. In production you often build a `ThreadPoolExecutor` directly with a bounded queue instead.

`Executors.newCachedThreadPool()` creates a pool with zero core threads, a maximum of `Integer.MAX_VALUE`, and a `SynchronousQueue` that hands each task directly to a thread. Idle threads are reaped after 60 seconds. It shines for large numbers of short, independent tasks that arrive in bursts, because threads are reused and the pool shrinks when quiet. For example, firing off many quick notification calls: `Executors.newCachedThreadPool().submit(() -> sendSms("CUR3001"))`. The danger is that it will create a new thread for every task that cannot immediately find a free one, so a flood of slow tasks creates an unbounded number of threads and can crash the JVM.

`Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(n)` returns a `ScheduledExecutorService` that can run tasks after a delay or on a repeating schedule. You use `schedule` for a one-off delay, `scheduleAtFixedRate` for runs every fixed period measured from the start of each run, and `scheduleWithFixedDelay` for runs with a fixed gap after each completion. For example, posting daily interest: `scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(this::postInterest, 0, 24, TimeUnit.HOURS)`. A key nuance is that fixed-rate runs can overlap or bunch up if a run takes longer than the period, whereas fixed-delay never overlaps.

`ForkJoinPool` is different in kind. Each worker thread has its own double-ended queue. When a task calls `fork`, the subtask is pushed onto the current thread's own deque; when a thread runs out of its own work it steals from the tail of another thread's deque. This work-stealing keeps all cores busy with minimal contention, which is why it is excellent for recursive divide-and-conquer such as summing a huge array of transaction amounts with a `RecursiveTask`. The common pool (used by parallel streams) sizes itself to roughly the number of cores.

```java
public class SumTask extends java.util.concurrent.RecursiveTask<Long> {
    private final long[] amounts; private final int lo, hi;
    SumTask(long[] a, int lo, int hi) { this.amounts = a; this.lo = lo; this.hi = hi; }
    protected Long compute() {
        if (hi - lo <= 1000) {                 // small enough: compute directly
            long s = 0; for (int i = lo; i < hi; i++) s += amounts[i]; return s;
        }
        int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;
        SumTask left = new SumTask(amounts, lo, mid);
        left.fork();                            // schedule left on my deque
        SumTask right = new SumTask(amounts, mid, hi);
        return right.compute() + left.join();   // compute right now, join left
    }
}
```

The reason fork/join is a poor choice for blocking IO is fundamental to its design. The pool is sized to roughly one thread per core on the assumption that threads are almost always running, not waiting. If a fork/join task blocks on a database or network call, that thread is stuck and cannot steal work or be stolen from, so cores sit idle and throughput collapses. The pool tries to compensate by spawning extra threads when it detects blocking through `ManagedBlocker`, but ordinary blocking calls do not trigger that mechanism, so you simply starve. For blocking IO you should use a fixed or appropriately sized `ThreadPoolExecutor` instead, never the common fork/join pool or a parallel stream.

